SECTION 1 — PROJECT PURPOSE AND DEVELOPMENT VISION
1.1 The multiplayer project
Develop a complete, functional online multiplayer experience for THE WORLD HAS ROTTEN.

THE WORLD HAS ROTTEN is a futuristic science-fiction survival horror game set around 3059–3062 AD. The world has been devastated by meteor impacts carrying alien organisms known as THEY. These organisms have invaded the planet and corrupted its advanced technology.

THEY infect metals and rare-earth elements only. They do not infect humans, animals, trees, grass, vines or other plants. Infected machinery, weapons, electronics, military equipment and mechs can transform into terrifying biomechanical structures combining steel, mechanical skeletons, exposed organic tissue, tendons, muscles and parasite growths.

THEY have also gained control of humanity's satellite network. They use the satellites to transmit a powerful signal through space, summoning their forces to conquer Earth.

Humanity has advanced technology, weapons, military equipment and mechs, but the invasion has caused widespread destruction. Players must explore abandoned locations, restore power, investigate the invasion, survive hostile creatures, uncover the truth and eventually attempt to stop the signal.

The multiplayer mode must allow players to experience this world together.

It must not feel like a separate, simplified game. It must preserve the atmosphere, story, monsters, environments, gameplay systems, graphics direction and survival horror identity of the original project.

The multiplayer experience must be built around cooperation, tension, exploration, communication, shared danger and meaningful teamwork.

1.2 Primary development goal
Create a multiplayer system that allows players to:

Create online sessions.

Invite friends.

Join private games.

Find public games where supported.

Play the campaign cooperatively.

Explore the same world.

Fight the same monsters.

Share mission objectives.

Activate generators together.

Complete puzzles.

Collect resources.

Help injured teammates.

Fight bosses cooperatively.

Experience synchronised cinematics.

Progress through the campaign.

Play a separate survival mode.

Communicate through supported voice or text systems.

Save progress reliably.

Reconnect after connection interruptions where supported.

The system must be genuinely multiplayer. Displaying another player's character model in the world is not sufficient.

Every major multiplayer feature must work through real network communication and shared game-state management.

1.3 Recommended player count
Build the initial multiplayer architecture around two-player cooperative gameplay.

After two-player functionality is stable, expand the system to four players.

The main cooperative campaign should target two to four players.

Do not begin by attempting to implement a very large lobby with eight, sixteen or more players. Larger sessions introduce additional networking, AI, performance, synchronisation and testing challenges.

Only introduce larger player counts if the engine, server infrastructure and actual game design can support them.

The player count must be configurable rather than scattered as hardcoded values throughout the project.

Create a central multiplayer configuration containing:

Minimum players.

Maximum players.

Maximum lobby size.

Maximum active players per mission.

Session timeout.

Reconnection window.

Network update settings.

Matchmaking limits.

Supported game modes.

All limits must be validated by the authoritative session system.

SECTION 2 — MULTIPLAYER GAME MODES
2.1 Cooperative campaign
The main multiplayer experience must be a cooperative version of the established ten-stage campaign.

The stages are:

The Fallen City.

The Silent Estates.

The Dead Mall.

The Dead Construction Zone.

The Underground.

The Forgotten Laboratory.

The Iron Graveyard.

Ground Zero.

The Titanforged.

The Final Signal.

Players must be able to complete the campaign together.

The campaign must preserve its existing story progression. Multiplayer must not create contradictory events or allow different players in the same session to see incompatible versions of the story.

Every player should participate in the important events, including:

Discovering clues.

Finding important equipment.

Activating generators.

Opening locked routes.

Discovering secret areas.

Encountering new monsters.

Surviving major attacks.

Completing mission objectives.

Fighting bosses.

Watching stage-ending cinematics.

Discovering the next stage.

Some exploration can be optional. However, critical progression must be controlled by the shared mission system.

For example, if the team must activate three generators, the mission must recognise the team's combined progress. It must not require every player to activate all three generators independently.

2.2 Cooperative survival mode
Create a separate survival mode for players who want to survive repeated monster encounters.

The mode should use locations and environments from the established game where appropriate.

Possible locations include:

Abandoned city streets.

Destroyed shopping centres.

Residential districts.

Construction zones.

Underground tunnels.

Research facilities.

Military installations.

Meteor impact zones.

Players must work together to survive increasingly difficult encounters.

Survival mode may include:

Enemy waves.

Limited ammunition.

Resource collection.

Optional objectives.

Generator restoration.

Defendable locations.

Environmental hazards.

Elite monsters.

Boss encounters.

Team survival scoring.

Each wave should have a defined difficulty profile. Avoid creating difficulty by simply multiplying enemy health without improving the encounter design.

Use different monster combinations and attack patterns to create variety.

For example, a wave might combine a fast hunter with a slower heavy attacker. The fast creature can pressure isolated players while the heavy creature controls the team's escape route.

Survival mode must remain fair. Players should receive understandable warnings before major threats appear.

2.3 Private multiplayer sessions
Players must be able to create private sessions for friends.

The private lobby should provide:

Create session.

Invite friends.

Accept invitations.

Join session.

Leave session.

Display connected players.

Display player readiness.

Display connection status.

Select game mode.

Select available campaign stage.

Select difficulty.

Start the game.

Return to the lobby.

The host or authorised session leader may control protected settings.

A normal player must not be able to change the selected stage or start the mission without the required permissions.

If the host leaves, the system must follow a defined host-leaving policy. If host migration is supported, transfer session authority safely. If it is not supported, clearly notify players and close or preserve the session according to the chosen architecture.

Do not silently transfer authority to an arbitrary player without validating the new session state.

2.4 Public matchmaking
Implement public matchmaking if the chosen platform and online infrastructure support it.

Players should be able to search for sessions based on:

Game mode.

Campaign stage.

Difficulty.

Region.

Available player slots.

Connection quality.

Relevant progression requirements.

Matchmaking should avoid placing players into incompatible campaign states.

For example, a player who has not unlocked a particular stage should not automatically be placed into a session that would corrupt their campaign progression.

If a player joins a later-stage session through an allowed feature, record the distinction between the session's progress and the player's permanent campaign unlocks.

Matchmaking must have clear states:

Searching.

Finding a suitable session.

Connecting.

Joining.

Waiting for the session to begin.

Failed or cancelled.

Provide meaningful error messages for connection failures, full lobbies, incompatible versions and unavailable services.

Do not leave players staring at an endless loading screen.

SECTION 3 — MULTIPLAYER SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE
3.1 Architectural requirements
Before implementing multiplayer gameplay, inspect the existing project.

Identify:

The game engine.

The current player controller.

The existing character and monster systems.

The physics engine.

The existing AI architecture.

The inventory system.

