Available platform services.

Do not replace working systems without a technical reason.

Prefer extending the existing project architecture rather than creating duplicate systems that conflict with it.

Document which systems already exist, which need modification and which need to be built from scratch.

3.2 Network authority
Use an authoritative networking model appropriate to the selected engine and deployment environment.

A dedicated authoritative server is suitable if the project can support it. A host-authoritative cooperative model may be acceptable for an initial prototype.

Regardless of the chosen architecture, define exactly which machine or service is responsible for each important game decision.

Authoritative systems should manage:

Session state.

Mission progression.

Generator completion.

Player damage.

Monster damage.

Boss health.

Monster deaths.

Important item collection.

Inventory validation.

Objective completion.

Player revival.

Campaign saves.

Clients may predict or display immediate feedback, but important outcomes must be validated.

For example, when a player shoots a monster, the local client may immediately show a muzzle flash and play the firing sound. The authoritative system must still validate the shot and determine the actual damage outcome.

Do not allow clients to arbitrarily announce that a boss has been defeated.

3.3 Separate networking responsibilities
Organise the multiplayer code into clear systems.

Recommended logical modules:

Session Manager

Creates, joins, updates and closes multiplayer sessions.

Lobby Manager

Controls lobby membership, readiness, game settings and invitations.

Player Network Manager

Tracks player connections, network identities, movement and state.

Game State Manager

Maintains the authoritative shared state of the current match.

Mission Manager

Tracks objectives, mission events and stage completion.

Monster Network Manager

Synchronises monster states and authoritative AI decisions.

Combat Manager

Validates weapons, damage, attacks, hits and deaths.

Inventory Manager

Manages player inventory and any shared resources.

Generator Manager

Controls generator interaction and shared puzzle progress.

Cinematic Manager

Coordinates shared cutscenes and story transitions.

Save Manager

Stores campaign progress and restores valid session state.

Communication Manager

Manages supported voice, text and player communication features.

These modules should communicate through documented interfaces rather than depending on uncontrolled direct references to one another.

3.4 Network identities
Every connected player must have a unique network identity within the session.

Do not use a character's display name as the only identifier.

Track:

Network player ID.

Platform identity where available.

Session identity.

Display name.

Connection status.

Selected character.

Player readiness.

Current health.

Current state.

Last confirmed session activity.

Validate identity whenever a client requests an important action.

Prevent duplicate player instances when reconnecting.

A reconnecting player should recover their existing session identity when the architecture permits it, rather than being treated as an entirely new player with a duplicate character.

3.5 Network messages and events
Create a clear event system for important multiplayer actions.

Examples include:

PlayerJoined.

PlayerLeft.

PlayerReadyChanged.

MissionStarted.

ObjectiveUpdated.

GeneratorProgressChanged.

GeneratorActivated.

MonsterDetectedPlayer.

