Do not assume that every network message arrives immediately or in the order expected.

The system must handle delayed, repeated or missing messages safely.

SECTION 4 — PLAYER MOVEMENT AND SYNCHRONISATION
4.1 Movement requirements
All players must see one another moving naturally.

Synchronise the relevant movement states:

Idle.

Walking.

Running.

Sprinting.

Jumping.

Falling.

Landing.

Crouching.

Climbing where supported.

Interacting.

Aiming.

Shooting.

Reloading.

Melee attacks.

Taking damage.

Downed state.

Reviving.

Death.

The remote character must display the correct animation for the action being performed.

Avoid situations where one player sees another player running while the other appears to slide across the ground.

4.2 Smooth movement
Use suitable network interpolation and prediction.

Remote player movement should be smoothed using received position and rotation updates.

Avoid directly snapping the remote character to every received network position.

For locally controlled players, use prediction where appropriate and reconcile authoritative updates without creating noticeable movement jumps.

Do not allow movement prediction to override important collision or gameplay restrictions.

When network conditions worsen, the system should degrade gracefully.

A slightly delayed remote animation is preferable to a character repeatedly teleporting across the environment.

4.3 Collision and physical consistency
The multiplayer game must preserve the global collision integrity requirements of the project.

Audit all ten stages and all multiplayer environments.

Fix:

Gaps between walls.

Missing floor collision.

Incorrect building collision.

Unintended openings.

Incorrect stairs.

Floating collision surfaces.

Overlapping collision meshes.

Players passing through solid props.

Monsters clipping through solid structures.

Physics objects entering inaccessible geometry.

Preserve intended routes, including doors, windows, corridors, stairs, vents and designed shortcuts.

Do not solve every collision problem by adding invisible boxes that obstruct legitimate movement.

Use collision meshes that accurately represent the intended physical structure.

Test all multiplayer movement states, including walking, sprinting, crouching, jumping, falling and interacting with objects.

4.4 Player separation and boundaries
The team should be able to explore independently within the mission's intended boundaries.

However, prevent players from travelling so far apart that mission logic, streaming or multiplayer synchronisation becomes unreliable.

Use level boundaries and mission rules rather than arbitrary invisible walls wherever possible.

If a player moves too far away from the team:

Display an understandable warning.

Show the direction of the team or mission area.

Provide time to return.

Apply a mission-specific boundary rule if necessary.

Do not teleport a player without clear warning and a legitimate gameplay reason.

SECTION 5 — COOPERATIVE GAMEPLAY AND TEAMWORK
5.1 Teamwork philosophy
The game should reward cooperation without making solo contribution impossible.

Players should have opportunities to help one another through combat, exploration, resource management and objectives.

Do not require every player to perform the same action at the same time unless the mission specifically calls for coordinated teamwork.

Create situations where players can naturally support each other.

Examples:

One player distracts a monster while another activates a generator.

One player watches a corridor while another searches a room.

One player collects ammunition while another protects the team.

Two players attack different weak points of a boss.

One player revives a teammate while another keeps enemies away.

Players coordinate when a storm reduces visibility.

Teammates communicate when a monster loses sight of them and begins searching.

5.2 Optional player roles
Provide flexible play styles rather than mandatory character classes.

Possible responsibilities include:

Combat-focused player: prioritises fighting monsters and protecting teammates.

Explorer: searches rooms, finds clues and identifies useful routes.

Support player: assists injured teammates and helps manage resources.

Scout: observes monster behaviour and identifies threats.

Objective specialist: focuses on generators, machinery and mission interactions.

These are suggestions for play styles, not restrictions.

Any player should be able to contribute to the mission.

If the project introduces character abilities, make sure no essential mission objective depends on one specific character unless that requirement is deliberately designed and explained.

5.3 Shared objectives
Create a central objective state.

All players must receive consistent information about:

Current main objective.

Completed objectives.

Remaining objectives.

Optional objectives.

Required equipment.

Important locations.

Mission progress.

If one player completes an objective, the update must be reflected for everyone.

Prevent duplicate completion rewards or duplicated mission triggers.

If two players interact with the same objective simultaneously, the authoritative system must resolve the interaction safely.

SECTION 6 — HEALTH, DAMAGE AND REVIVAL
6.1 Shared multiplayer damage rules
Player damage must be validated by the authoritative game system.

Synchronise:

Current health.

Damage received.

Damage source.

Status effects.

Healing.

Downed state.

Death.

Revival.

Respawn where permitted.

Players must not receive different versions of the same damage event.

Damage feedback should be responsive, but the final result must be authoritative.

6.2 Downed state
When a player reaches zero health, enter a defined downed state if the selected difficulty and game mode permit it.

The downed player should:

Play an appropriate animation.

Be unable to perform normal combat.

Receive clear visual feedback.

See a teammate assistance indicator.

Have a clearly displayed recovery or bleed-out timer if one is used.

Be able to communicate if the voice system permits it.

The player should not immediately disappear from the world unless the game design explicitly requires that behaviour.

6.3 Revival interaction
A teammate should be able to approach a downed player and begin a revival interaction.

The revival system must include:

Correct interaction range.

A visible progress indicator.

A defined revival duration.

Animation.

Sound feedback.

Interruption rules.

Successful completion.

Health restoration.

Multiplayer synchronisation.

If the reviving player moves away, receives an interrupting attack or otherwise breaks the interaction conditions, cancel or pause the revival according to the chosen design.

Prevent multiple players from completing duplicate revivals on the same teammate.

6.4 Team failure
Define what happens when all players are downed.

Possible outcomes include:

Restart from the latest checkpoint.

Restart the current encounter.

Return to the lobby.

Offer a limited recovery option.

Use the same clear rules for the whole team.

Do not delete campaign progression because of a temporary network error.

Keep checkpoint and failure behaviour consistent with the selected difficulty.

SECTION 7 — MONSTER AND BOSS MULTIPLAYER FOUNDATIONS
7.1 Shared monster state
Every monster must have one authoritative gameplay state.

Synchronise:

Position.

Rotation.

Animation state.

Health.

Target.

Detection state.

Attack state.

Damage reactions.

Stagger.

Death.

Destruction.

Relevant sound events.

Do not run conflicting independent versions of the same monster on different clients.

The authoritative system should determine important monster decisions.

Clients should receive enough information to display the monster smoothly and consistently.

7.2 Monster detection
Preserve the established monster detection rules.

Monsters must use genuine line-of-sight checks and hearing systems.

A monster should visually detect a player only when it has a valid view of that player.

Walls, buildings, closed doors and suitable cover should block visual detection.

If a player disappears behind cover:

The monster should lose direct visual contact.

It should remember the last seen position.

It should move towards that position.

It should search nearby areas.

It should investigate believable sound sources.

It should eventually resume patrol if it cannot find the player.

Do not allow monsters to know exact player positions through walls without a legitimate gameplay ability.

Hearing should provide clues based on actual sound events rather than perfect knowledge of player coordinates.

7.3 Physical monster movement
All monsters must obey physical collision rules.

They must not:

Phase through walls.

Teleport through floors.

Clip through solid doors.

Pass through buildings.

Ignore collision geometry.

Attack through solid objects without an appropriate attack mechanic.

Monsters must use valid routes and physical openings.

Large creatures should not fit through small vents or narrow doors.

Smaller creatures may use openings that genuinely fit their bodies.

Powerful monsters may break appropriate destructible barriers if their abilities and the environment support it.

7.4 Multiplayer boss encounters
Boss encounters must be shared events.

The boss's health, attack phases, target, weak points and defeat state must be synchronised.

Preserve the existing identities and designs of:

THE HOLLOW.

THE TITANFORGED.

THE COLOSSUS.

THE FORGE.

Do not redesign existing bosses while upgrading their multiplayer behaviour.

Every boss should have clearly readable attack preparation, execution and recovery.

Players must have opportunities to react, evade, cooperate and counterattack.

Do not allow network delay to make boss attacks appear to hit players before the animation or telegraph is visible.

Boss defeat events must be processed once and shared consistently.

SECTION 8 — GENERATOR AND OBJECTIVE NETWORKING
8.1 Generator rules must remain unchanged
Each generator requires ten different maths questions answered correctly.

Only multiplication and division are allowed.

Questions must be easy and have whole-number answers.

Division must be exact.

Never generate:

Decimal answers.

Fractions.

Remainders.

Complicated equations.

Unrelated question types.

Every correct answer adds 10% progress.

Wrong answers do not remove existing progress.

Players can retry indefinitely.

Every generator must have its own unique set of ten questions.

8.2 Multiplayer generator authority
Generator progress must be stored as a shared authoritative value.

All players must see the same percentage.

For example:

Initial progress: 0%.

First correct answer: 10%.

Fifth correct answer: 50%.

Eighth correct answer: 80%.

Tenth correct answer: 100%.

When progress reaches 100%, activate the generator exactly once.

If two players submit answers simultaneously, process each submission safely.

Prevent:

Duplicate answer submissions.

Double progress increases.

Progress above 100%.

Progress disappearing after reconnection.

Different generator states across players.

8.3 Generator cooperation
Players may take turns answering questions.

One player may guard the area while another operates the generator.

The system should not require every player to answer an equal number of questions.

The shared generator objective should be understandable and fair.

If a player disconnects during generator activation, preserve completed progress.

